Answer: It depends on how many times the statement needs to be executed and on the requirement of the problem. For example, if a statement has to execute five times from 1 to 5 in the forward direction:

```java
for(int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
System.out.println("String Stack");
}
```

Here: i = 1 → initialization i <= 5 → condition i++ → increment

Answer: A for loop is a looping control construct used to repeatedly execute a statement or block of statements. Its syntax is:

```java
for(initialization; condition; increment/decrement) {
// statements
}
```

For example:

```java
for(int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
System.out.println("String Stack");
}
```

Answer: ```java
for(int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
System.out.println("String Stack");
}
```

The execution happens as follows: i = 1 → condition true → print i = 2 → condition true → print i = 3 → condition true → print i = 4 → condition true → print i = 5 → condition true → print i = 6 → condition false → exit Therefore, String Stack is printed five times.

Answer: Suppose String Stack has to be printed five times. Without a loop, the statement would have to be written five times:

```java
System.out.println("String Stack");
System.out.println("String Stack");
System.out.println("String Stack");
System.out.println("String Stack");
System.out.println("String Stack");
```

Using a loop, the statement is written only once:

```java
for(int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
System.out.println("String Stack");
}
```

The statement is written once but executed five times.

Answer: For a loop such as:

```java
for(int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
System.out.println("String Stack");
}
```

the execution flow is: Initialize i to 1. Check the condition i <= 5. If the condition is true, execute the loop body. After completing the loop body, execute i++. Check the condition again. Repeat the process while the condition remains true. When the condition becomes false, exit the loop.

Answer: For forward execution, the loop variable is incremented. Example:

```java
for(int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
System.out.println("String Stack");
}
```

The loop variable takes the values: 1 2 3 4 5 The loop starts from 1, goes until 5, and moves forward using i++.

Answer: A decrement operator can be used when the loop needs to move backward. For example:

```java
for(int i = 1; i >= -3; i--) {
System.out.println("String Stack");
}
```

The loop variable takes the values: 1 0 -1 -2 -3 The loop starts at 1, goes until -3, and moves backward using i--.

Answer: If the condition is false during the first condition check, the loop body will not execute even once. For example:

```java
for(int i = 0; i <= -4; i++) {
System.out.println("String Stack");
}
```

The first condition is: 0 <= -4 which is false. Therefore, the loop does not execute even once.

Answer: An infinite loop is a loop whose condition remains true continuously, so the loop does not terminate normally. For example:

```java
for(int i = -1; i >= -5; i++) {
System.out.println("String Stack");
}
```

Here, i is incremented: -1 → 0 → 1 → 2 → 3 → ... The condition i >= -5 continues to remain true, so the loop keeps executing.

Answer: The increment or decrement must move the loop variable toward the point where the condition becomes false. If the loop variable moves in the wrong direction, the condition may never become false. For example:

```java
for(int i = -1; i >= -5; i++) {
System.out.println("String Stack");
}
```

The condition requires i to eventually become less than -5, but i++ keeps increasing the value. Therefore, the condition continues to remain true and the loop becomes infinite.

Answer: Writing a statement once means the source code contains that statement only one time. Executing it multiple times means the same statement is performed repeatedly during program execution. For example:

```java
for(int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
System.out.println("String Stack");
}
```

The println statement is written once but executed five times.

Answer: A while loop repeatedly executes a statement or block of statements as long as its condition is true. The initialization and increment/decrement are written separately from the while statement. Example:

```java
int i = 1;
while(i <= 5) {
System.out.println("String Stack");
i++;
}
```

It can be read as: Start from i = 1. As long as i <= 5, perform the task. After completing the task, increment i.

Answer: Both for and while loops can perform repetitive tasks and both involve initialization, condition checking, and increment/decrement. The main difference is the placement of these elements. In a for loop:

```java
for(int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
System.out.println("String Stack");
}
```

initialization, condition, and increment are written together in the loop syntax. In a while loop:

```java
int i = 1;
while(i <= 5) {
System.out.println("String Stack");
i++;
}
```

they are written separately.

Answer: Consider:

```java
int i = 1;
while(i <= 5) {
System.out.println("String Stack");
i++;
}
```

The execution is: i = 1 → 1 <= 5 → print → i becomes 2 i = 2 → 2 <= 5 → print → i becomes 3 i = 3 → 3 <= 5 → print → i becomes 4 i = 4 → 4 <= 5 → print → i becomes 5 i = 5 → 5 <= 5 → print → i becomes 6 i = 6 → 6 <= 5 → false → exit Therefore, the statement executes five times.

Answer: If the condition is false during the first check, the body of the while loop does not execute even once. For example:

```java
int i = 6;
while(i <= 5) {
System.out.println("String Stack");
i++;
}
```

The condition: 6 <= 5 is false. Therefore, the statement is not executed.

Answer: A do-while loop executes the loop body first and checks the condition afterward. Example:

```java
int i = 1;
do {
System.out.println("String Stack");
i++;
} while(i <= 5);
```

It can be read as: Start from i = 1 and do the task. After doing the task, continue as long as i <= 5.

Answer: Even if the condition is false after the first execution, the body of the do-while loop executes once because the condition is checked after the body. For example:

```java
int i = 10;
do {
System.out.println("String Stack");
i++;
} while(i <= 5);
```

The statement is printed once. After that, i becomes 11. The condition: 11 <= 5 is false, so the loop terminates.

Answer: A do-while loop places the task before the condition. For example:

```java
do {
System.out.println("String Stack");
i++;
} while(i <= 5);
```

The control first executes the do block without checking the condition. Only after completing the task does it check the while condition. Therefore, the body executes at least once.

Answer: Consider:

```java
int i = 10;
do {
System.out.println("String Stack");
i++;
} while(i <= 5);
```

Execution: i = 10 ↓ Execute the task ↓ Print "String Stack" ↓ i becomes 11 ↓ Check 11 <= 5 ↓ Condition is false ↓ Exit the loop Therefore, String Stack is printed once.

Answer: The task still executes once because the condition is checked only after the do block has executed. For example:

```java
int i = 1000;
do {
System.out.println("String Stack");
i++;
} while(i <= 5);
```

The statement is printed once, then the condition is checked and found to be false.

Answer: Debugging allows you to observe how the loop variable changes during execution. For example, with:

```java
for(int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
System.out.println("String Stack");
}
```

you can observe: i = 1 i = 2 i = 3 i = 4 i = 5 i = 6 At i = 6, the condition i <= 5 becomes false and the loop exits. This makes the control flow of the loop easier to understand.
